  "review": {
    "summary": "I found no substantive defects in WP-10 within the supplied scope (src/lib/auth.ts and src/lib/auth.test.ts, snapshot df4c52c3…). The new check is placed correctly in requireFinanceAdmin:\n\n1. requireAuth runs first, so an administrator who has a factor but hasn't verified in this session is still sent to /mfa/verify. This is the existing enrolled flow and it hasn't changed.\n2. The non-admin check then sends users without the platform administrator role to /dashboard, as before.\n3. Only then does an administrator with no factor get redirected to /mfa/setup. A stale mfaVerifiedAt can't skip this, because the check uses mfaFactor, not the timestamp.\n\nThe setup page and the enrollment functions use requireAuth, which only redirects when a factor exists. So there's no setup loop, and the unit test confirms requireAuth still allows the unenrolled administrator through. Merchant users, session storage and merchant MFA policy are unaffected. Because the admin layout, individual pages and finance actions/route handlers all share requireFinanceAdmin, they all get the same protection.\n\nRemaining risks I couldn't check from the supplied text (assumptions, not defects):\n- **Pending factors:** this assumes user.mfaFactor is only set once enrollment is confirmed. If starting enrollment saves a pending factor to that same field, the code comment's 'confirmed factor' wording would need a confirmed-state check.\n- **Other admin guards:** this assumes no other platform-admin entry point or guard skips requireFinanceAdmin.\n- **Fixed redirect target:** the next parameter is always /admin/billing/dashboard. That's a usability limitation, not a safety issue.\n\nAs you said, the browser evidence doesn't cover full enrollment completion end to end. No findings here doesn't mean tests or the release have passed.",
    "findings": []
  },
